WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.795
Whenever you listen to the radio,
watch TV, or browse the Internet,

00:00:04.795 --> 00:00:07.880
you are bombarded with
crises of all types.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.691
While these crises, political, health,
social, play out on a global stage,

00:00:12.691 --> 00:00:16.500
many of us struggle with
crisis situations of our own.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.840
Today, I look forward to our
discussion of crisis communication.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.136
I'm Matt Abrahams, and

00:00:22.136 --> 00:00:26.092
I teach strategic communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:30.485
Welcome to Think Fast,
Talk Smart, the podcast.

00:00:30.485 --> 00:00:33.516
I am so
excited to be joined by David Demarest,

00:00:33.516 --> 00:00:36.332
who is a lecturer of
management at the GSB.

00:00:36.332 --> 00:00:40.994
Prior to the GSB, David served as
White House Communications Director for

00:00:40.994 --> 00:00:42.702
President George HW Bush.

00:00:42.702 --> 00:00:45.550
And was a member of
the White House senior staff.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.266
Next, David served as
Executive Vice President and

00:00:48.266 --> 00:00:51.820
Director of Corporate Communications
at Bank of America.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.212
And then as Executive Vice President for
Corporate Relations and

00:00:55.212 --> 00:00:58.250
Brand Management at Visa International.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.299
Later, he became Stanford's Vice President
for Public Affairs and

00:01:02.299 --> 00:01:06.087
oversaw communications and
government relations and events.

00:01:06.087 --> 00:01:07.904
Beyond teaching at the GSB,

00:01:07.904 --> 00:01:12.913
David is finishing his book about what
life is like working in the White House.

00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:14.182
Welcome, David.

00:01:14.182 --> 00:01:15.119
&gt;&gt; Hey, thanks, Matt.

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:17.356
It's great to be with you,
this is going to be fun.

00:01:17.356 --> 00:01:20.306
&gt;&gt; There's so [LAUGH] much for
us to discuss, shall we get started?

00:01:20.306 --> 00:01:21.205
&gt;&gt; You bet.

00:01:21.205 --> 00:01:24.250
&gt;&gt; So question one,
when it comes to crises,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.051
you've had to deal with a few for sure.

00:01:27.051 --> 00:01:30.123
Can you highlight one or
two that you've dealt with, and

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:33.404
any learnings that have come
from those experiences?

00:01:33.404 --> 00:01:34.417
&gt;&gt; Well, sure.

00:01:34.417 --> 00:01:40.694
One day, I came into the office and
one of my staff told me that we had,

00:01:40.694 --> 00:01:45.782
due to a data processing center foulup,
that we had lost

00:01:45.782 --> 00:01:51.325
1.2 million automatic credits and
automatic deposits.

00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:55.024
That's a lot of transactions to lose.

00:01:55.024 --> 00:01:56.927
And they were all in California.

00:01:56.927 --> 00:02:02.430
And it meant that people's mortgage
payments weren't going to happen.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:06.005
It meant that their payroll,
their paycheck didn't get deposited,

00:02:06.005 --> 00:02:07.200
things like that.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.739
It meant that a lot of insufficient
funds checks might be going out,

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:13.743
and it was a incredible mess.

00:02:13.743 --> 00:02:18.376
This was a formula for
a whole lot of very angry customers.

00:02:18.376 --> 00:02:18.888
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:23.668
&gt;&gt; Well, what we were able
to do was to disaggregate,

00:02:23.668 --> 00:02:30.128
what were the accounts that might
have been more easily remediated?

00:02:30.128 --> 00:02:34.986
By virtue of if somebody had a Bank of
America account for their payroll deposit,

00:02:34.986 --> 00:02:38.486
and a Bank of America account for
their mortgage payment,

00:02:38.486 --> 00:02:42.640
we could intercept that
transaction in between and fix it.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.984
What it meant was that we could get those
transactions down to a manageable level,

00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:52.280
of the ones that we really couldn't
address in the short-term.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.343
So by the end of the day,
we had narrowed it down to about

00:02:55.343 --> 00:02:59.650
69,000 insufficient funds notices
that were going to go out.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.006
So that meant that our focus could be,
okay,

00:03:02.006 --> 00:03:04.650
what are we going to
do about those people?

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.757
And come up with a remedy for
them that made sense, and

00:03:08.757 --> 00:03:12.860
get our apology notes out to them and
so forth.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.097
It told me that you need to be able
to disaggregate the problem into its

00:03:17.097 --> 00:03:21.210
component parts, and deal with
the parts that are most critical.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:27.041
The second crisis I
would put out there was,

00:03:27.041 --> 00:03:31.280
at Stanford, we had a laptop,
it was stolen.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.617
And it contained a lot of
employee personal data,

00:03:34.617 --> 00:03:38.520
like thousands of
employees' personal data.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.063
We didn't know if it was stolen for
the data, or

00:03:41.063 --> 00:03:45.740
if it was just stolen because it was
a laptop, that didn't really matter.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:49.243
By the time we figured out
what was on the computer,

00:03:49.243 --> 00:03:54.306
we knew that we had to do a public
announcement, too, as a notification.

00:03:54.306 --> 00:03:57.044
But it was already late
in the day on Friday,

00:03:57.044 --> 00:04:00.459
not the time when you want
to do a public announcement.

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:03.308
People think you're trying
to hide the information.

00:04:03.308 --> 00:04:07.570
So our choice really was to do it then or
do it Monday.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:12.050
Also on Monday, we'd have all the systems
in place, the toll free number,

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:16.890
what we're going to do about
identity theft and things like that.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:20.419
We made the choice to
go on Friday at 6 PM.

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:25.930
And we did that because we
felt that people's trust

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:31.834
in us trumped the efficiency
of waiting until Monday.

00:04:31.834 --> 00:04:36.749
That if anyone had their identity stolen
over the weekend, and they found out

00:04:36.749 --> 00:04:41.132
that we knew about this breach, but
we didn't talk to them about it,

00:04:41.132 --> 00:04:45.087
when we knew about it,
that would be a violation of the trust.

00:04:45.087 --> 00:04:49.840
And we came down to
trust beats efficiency.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.530
And in our communication,

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:56.580
we told people that we won't have
the toll free number set up until Monday.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.701
We won't have the identity theft
remediation set up until Monday.

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:06.574
But we felt it was important to get
this information out as soon as we

00:05:06.574 --> 00:05:09.017
knew what the circumstance was.

00:05:09.017 --> 00:05:13.768
Because we didn't want to risk
someone not being able to take

00:05:13.768 --> 00:05:17.876
an action on their own to
try to protect themselves.

00:05:17.876 --> 00:05:22.020
&gt;&gt; Thank you for sharing those two,
I hear two lessons that you learned.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:25.062
One is, in a big, complicated crisis,

00:05:25.062 --> 00:05:30.260
it's to your advantage to break it
down into its constituent parts.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:34.272
And it sounds like having a platform in
technology that can help you doesn't hurt

00:05:34.272 --> 00:05:35.700
in that situation.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:41.177
And then the other piece is to know your
values that you as an organization have,

00:05:41.177 --> 00:05:45.340
and really challenge yourself
to live by those values.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:48.202
And that must help be a guiding light.

00:05:48.202 --> 00:05:55.926
&gt;&gt; I think too many organizations haven't
spent enough time really thinking about,

00:05:55.926 --> 00:06:01.219
what would they do in a circumstance
that called upon them

00:06:01.219 --> 00:06:07.600
to act without knowing all of
the details of the situation?

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.330
The pressure to act can be intense, and

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:13.823
that's why knowing your values is so
important.

00:06:13.823 --> 00:06:18.522
Because it gives you
a beacon that can inform how

00:06:18.522 --> 00:06:23.370
you're going to prioritize your actions.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:27.819
&gt;&gt; So let me ask you the second question,
when communicating about a crisis,

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:30.510
what are some best
practices to keep in mind?

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:34.069
So when you're actually
sending the message out.

00:06:34.069 --> 00:06:39.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think the key here is that,
A, you gotta know what you don't know.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:45.346
So you have to have kind of a catalogue of
information that you would like to know,

00:06:45.346 --> 00:06:46.890
but maybe you don't know.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:51.970
And how important is that information,
which leads to, don't speculate.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:57.989
People speculate as to what might be the
case, not necessarily what is the case.

00:06:57.989 --> 00:07:02.225
And then, you mentioned it earlier,
know your organization's values and

00:07:02.225 --> 00:07:03.294
be guided by them.

00:07:03.294 --> 00:07:07.915
Companies and organizations that have
a solid sense of who they are and

00:07:07.915 --> 00:07:09.332
what they stand for.

00:07:09.332 --> 00:07:13.412
They will be able to navigate
a crisis much more effectively than

00:07:13.412 --> 00:07:16.525
those companies or
organizations that don't.

00:07:16.525 --> 00:07:21.307
And I even tell my students this, Matt,
that if you know as a person what

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:25.453
your values are in this crazy
turbulent world that we live in,

00:07:25.453 --> 00:07:30.424
you'll be able to navigate it a lot
better than to always be situational.

00:07:30.424 --> 00:07:35.010
Finally, if there's a necessity for
apology, and often there is,

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:39.460
that apology has to be timely,
it's got to show true regret.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.544
The organization has to take real
responsibility, in other words, ownership,

00:07:43.544 --> 00:07:45.690
and if possible, provide a remedy.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.805
And that's what we did in
the case of the stolen laptop.

00:07:48.805 --> 00:07:52.651
We did provide a remedy to people,
which was identity theft protection, and

00:07:52.651 --> 00:07:53.729
we did that quickly.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:56.112
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:07:56.112 --> 00:08:01.584
So I know you have some very specific ways
of thinking about audience engagement,

00:08:01.584 --> 00:08:04.482
can you share what this looks like?

00:08:04.482 --> 00:08:06.083
From your perspective?

00:08:06.083 --> 00:08:09.427
&gt;&gt; I think that,
if we take Stanford as an example, but

00:08:09.427 --> 00:08:12.324
it applies to pretty
much any organization.

00:08:12.324 --> 00:08:16.261
All organizations have
multiple constituencies.

00:08:16.261 --> 00:08:20.198
So in Stanford's case,
as you well know, we have students,

00:08:20.198 --> 00:08:23.471
we have faculty,
we have donors, we have alumni.

00:08:23.471 --> 00:08:25.780
We have the federal government,

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:31.228
who supplies us with $1 billion of
research every year, research funding.

00:08:31.228 --> 00:08:34.161
We have the local community and so forth.

00:08:34.161 --> 00:08:39.034
And so every organization has multiple
constituencies that care about

00:08:39.034 --> 00:08:43.676
how the organization interacts with them,
some more than others.

00:08:43.676 --> 00:08:48.865
So part of effective crisis management
is to have your finger on the pulse

00:08:48.865 --> 00:08:54.479
of each one of those constituencies, and
what elements of the crisis are going to

00:08:54.479 --> 00:08:59.178
be germane to each constituency,
to a greater or lesser degree.

00:08:59.178 --> 00:09:03.213
So you start to have kind of
a stakeholder map, in a sense.

00:09:03.213 --> 00:09:08.490
And then that allows you to know what
their concerns, what their fears are,

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:11.675
what their anger quotient might be-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:09:11.675 --> 00:09:13.883
&gt;&gt; If something has affected them very

00:09:13.883 --> 00:09:14.624
directly.

00:09:14.624 --> 00:09:18.401
And then the next step
is to go where they are.

00:09:18.401 --> 00:09:22.414
You can't kind of sit back and
wait for them to come to you.

00:09:22.414 --> 00:09:26.678
You have to go to them and
basically listen to them, ask for

00:09:26.678 --> 00:09:30.856
their guidance as to what it
is that is of concern to them,

00:09:30.856 --> 00:09:34.268
and how can they help
you meet their concerns?

00:09:34.268 --> 00:09:39.059
So it's a much more proactive
engagement with stakeholders than to

00:09:39.059 --> 00:09:43.690
simply tell people what you're doing and
then hope for the best.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:48.465
&gt;&gt; Right, so your audience engagement
approach is similar to what we've

00:09:48.465 --> 00:09:52.126
talked about prior on this podcast,
which is really,

00:09:52.126 --> 00:09:57.079
know your audiences and understand
what it is that's important to them.

00:09:57.079 --> 00:10:02.047
You're doing this very proactively,
in case something bad happens,

00:10:02.047 --> 00:10:05.292
you know and
you're engaged with them prior.

00:10:05.292 --> 00:10:10.590
And I also heard you say, be very
mindful of the different methodologies,

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:15.047
tools, and modalities with which
you can connect to them, and

00:10:15.047 --> 00:10:20.285
leverage those so you can get your
messages out quickly and proactively.

00:10:20.285 --> 00:10:24.709
I need to figure out how I can
affect the anger quotient of some of

00:10:24.709 --> 00:10:27.479
the people that I live with in my house.

00:10:27.479 --> 00:10:30.231
I'd like to reduce it a little bit, so

00:10:30.231 --> 00:10:33.505
you've got me thinking about that for
sure.

00:10:33.505 --> 00:10:38.544
We've talked about knowing your values,
knowing your corporate values.

00:10:38.544 --> 00:10:42.856
We've talked about knowing your
audience and engaging them.

00:10:42.856 --> 00:10:46.912
What are other things people can
do proactively to prepare for

00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:48.709
a crisis that might arise?

00:10:48.709 --> 00:10:51.114
&gt;&gt; Well, I'd say two things.

00:10:51.114 --> 00:10:56.044
One, there are certain crises that
happen that are impossible to foresee

00:10:56.044 --> 00:10:57.725
in very concrete terms.

00:10:57.725 --> 00:11:02.428
And people aren't going to blame
Stanford if there's an earthquake.

00:11:02.428 --> 00:11:06.468
What they will blame Stanford for
is if we were not prepared,

00:11:06.468 --> 00:11:09.412
knowing that we're in an earthquake zone.

00:11:09.412 --> 00:11:13.066
When I think about how
people think about crises,

00:11:13.066 --> 00:11:16.372
I would suggest that
they do an inventory of,

00:11:16.372 --> 00:11:22.291
what are the worst things that can happen,
and are we prepared for those things?

00:11:22.291 --> 00:11:26.607
That's kind of part one, part two is,
don't drink the Kool-Aid.

00:11:26.607 --> 00:11:28.971
A lot of people in organizations,

00:11:28.971 --> 00:11:33.538
particularly leadership in organizations,
fall into the trap of

00:11:33.538 --> 00:11:38.263
having an organizational bias
in favor of their organization.

00:11:38.263 --> 00:11:41.528
We would never do that,
that is a dangerous trap.

00:11:41.528 --> 00:11:44.848
We have to really get to
the facts of this case,

00:11:44.848 --> 00:11:50.825
not just what are our preconceived notions
are about how good our organization is.

00:11:50.825 --> 00:11:55.467
So you have to put yourself in
the shoes of someone who might have

00:11:55.467 --> 00:11:58.715
an alternative view of your organization.

00:11:58.715 --> 00:12:03.617
And that takes a little bit of hard
thinking, because I love working for

00:12:03.617 --> 00:12:06.735
Stanford, and I've been there 16 years.

00:12:06.735 --> 00:12:11.625
And it would be easy to think, well,
Stanford would never do this or

00:12:11.625 --> 00:12:12.680
never do that.

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:17.695
Well, you've got to be able to put
yourself in the shoes of somebody who

00:12:17.695 --> 00:12:23.139
might not think the way I do about
the university, or about my organization.

00:12:23.139 --> 00:12:26.964
Sometimes you've gotta
think like an adversary, or

00:12:26.964 --> 00:12:29.433
you have to think like a reporter.

00:12:29.433 --> 00:12:32.877
And get yourself out of your comfort zone,
and

00:12:32.877 --> 00:12:35.890
think in ways that a critic would think.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:40.984
&gt;&gt; Very useful ideas, and in fact,
the last idea, about seeing the situation

00:12:40.984 --> 00:12:45.921
from somebody else's perspective,
echoes something Bob Sutton shared

00:12:45.921 --> 00:12:50.637
on our podcast in terms of team
meetings and group decision-making.

00:12:50.637 --> 00:12:55.565
It can always benefit to have that
other perspective, and as you said,

00:12:55.565 --> 00:12:59.274
to make sure that we're
not drinking the Kool-Aid.

00:12:59.274 --> 00:13:02.625
And I think,
reflecting on what could go wrong,

00:13:02.625 --> 00:13:07.612
while maybe depressing and concerning,
is a great thing to do, again,

00:13:07.612 --> 00:13:10.996
in the name of being proactive and
preparation.

00:13:10.996 --> 00:13:13.873
I'd like to change the topic a little bit.

00:13:13.873 --> 00:13:18.081
You've spent a lot of your life
in the political world, and

00:13:18.081 --> 00:13:21.882
you even teach a course on
political communication.

00:13:21.882 --> 00:13:24.414
Looking at our current political
landscape, what advice do you have for

00:13:24.414 --> 00:13:27.028
people who are trying to push a political
agenda that is important to them,

00:13:27.028 --> 00:13:29.463
such as equity, energy, employment,
what advice might you have?

00:13:29.463 --> 00:13:36.728
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we live in a remarkably
polarized environment, politically.

00:13:36.728 --> 00:13:42.635
And it is very difficult
to have conversations,

00:13:42.635 --> 00:13:47.253
metaphorically, across the aisle.

00:13:47.253 --> 00:13:50.623
I think it's a tough
arena to navigate within.

00:13:50.623 --> 00:13:55.992
But I think, if you're going to be
successful in trying to expand support for

00:13:55.992 --> 00:14:01.195
your cause or your agenda, you have to
refrain from the temptation of being

00:14:01.195 --> 00:14:06.669
too judgmental about those who might not
buy into what you're trying to sell.

00:14:06.669 --> 00:14:12.441
And try to understand why, at a deeper
level, what's their concern about it?

00:14:12.441 --> 00:14:15.718
And then make your case on their terms,
not yours.

00:14:15.718 --> 00:14:20.535
You've gotta be thoughtful about
what avenue you can take to try to

00:14:20.535 --> 00:14:25.706
break through some of, perhaps,
a rigidity of view on the other side.

00:14:25.706 --> 00:14:31.014
But coming at it from a different angle
might be a little bit more persuasive

00:14:31.014 --> 00:14:36.337
than simply jumping up and down and
saying, you're the devil incarnate.

00:14:36.337 --> 00:14:41.468
The second half of that is, for
your supporters, it's really about moving

00:14:41.468 --> 00:14:47.179
them from just being supportive to action,
and that means communicating urgency.

00:14:47.179 --> 00:14:51.275
And how you communicate urgency
is to make sure that the see

00:14:51.275 --> 00:14:55.800
the present-day merits of dealing
with a particular problem.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:59.060
&gt;&gt; I really like how you
separate those two points.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:03.901
The thing that really resonated
with me was the notion initially of

00:15:03.901 --> 00:15:06.667
framing your point in the language and

00:15:06.667 --> 00:15:11.696
the approach that the people you're
trying to convince are taking.

00:15:11.696 --> 00:15:16.575
Rather than, it's my idea, and
you should see the world the way I do,

00:15:16.575 --> 00:15:21.042
and try to appreciate the way
the other people see the world, and

00:15:21.042 --> 00:15:24.290
put your communication
in that perspective.

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:29.310
And then the second part there,
this notion of urgency and driving action.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:33.338
A lot of feel that, hey,
if I just share the information,

00:15:33.338 --> 00:15:37.711
then it's inherent and
intuitive that action should follow.

00:15:37.711 --> 00:15:42.507
But building that sense of urgency,
providing a pathway to action,

00:15:42.507 --> 00:15:44.336
becomes very important.

00:15:44.336 --> 00:15:47.802
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I remember back
at my Bank of America days,

00:15:47.802 --> 00:15:51.530
the CFO was the person that
I ultimately reported to.

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:55.597
And when I would talk to him
about reputation management,

00:15:55.597 --> 00:16:00.660
he'd kind of glaze over, because I wasn't
talking in a language that he could

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:06.510
really relate to So, I changed my
language to be one about risk management.

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:09.520
That was a concept that he
was very familiar with.

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:14.654
And that enabled me to have more
fruitful conversations with him about

00:16:14.654 --> 00:16:19.962
the steps that we need to take when we
had to deal with reputational risk,

00:16:19.962 --> 00:16:23.545
business risk,
financial risk, and so forth.

00:16:23.545 --> 00:16:27.779
&gt;&gt; That last point you made about
the linguistics of it leads me to my next

00:16:27.779 --> 00:16:32.889
question, which I know you and I share as
something that we are passionate about and

00:16:32.889 --> 00:16:34.934
concerned with and teach about.

00:16:34.934 --> 00:16:40.290
And that is the language you use and
the rhetoric involved in communication.

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:44.600
What thoughts do you have of
the importance of our words?

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:48.702
And does anything change as a result
of our communicating virtually,

00:16:48.702 --> 00:16:50.240
rather than in-person?

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
those are really two great questions.

00:16:54.530 --> 00:17:00.701
I love language, one of the modules in
my class is about language and rhetoric.

00:17:00.701 --> 00:17:04.031
When you think about language and
you look at some,

00:17:04.031 --> 00:17:08.368
I don't mean to get too esoteric here,
but the word strategy, for

00:17:08.368 --> 00:17:13.107
example, comes from the Greek word
strategos, which means a general.

00:17:13.107 --> 00:17:19.494
And the word tactics comes from the Greek
tactos, which means arranged in order.

00:17:19.494 --> 00:17:22.374
The difference between strategy and
tactics then,

00:17:22.374 --> 00:17:26.580
you kind of can bring to life by
talking about their derivation.

00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:30.108
So, language is really what we
use to provide information,

00:17:30.108 --> 00:17:35.440
to make an argument, to motivate behavior,
paint a picture, tell a story.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:39.710
All of those things are essential
to bringing your issue to life.

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:43.640
And in other words, first building
an understanding of one's agenda.

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:47.120
I think, generally speaking,

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:50.207
those principles are applicable
to the virtual world.

00:17:50.207 --> 00:17:52.657
Crisis management is, indeed,

00:17:52.657 --> 00:17:58.084
much more complicated with the advent
of social media, we all know that.

00:17:58.084 --> 00:18:03.639
Speed is essential because social
media creates a narrative so

00:18:03.639 --> 00:18:07.720
quickly that people can mobilize faster.

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:10.420
Bad news travels faster than it used to.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:12.888
Misinformation can spread like wildfire.

00:18:12.888 --> 00:18:17.801
You have to understand the platforms
that we are all living with and

00:18:17.801 --> 00:18:21.935
you've gotta communicate
to people in those spheres.

00:18:21.935 --> 00:18:25.411
It used to be a rule,
kind of a communications rule,

00:18:25.411 --> 00:18:29.443
that never repeat the accusation
that's made against you.

00:18:29.443 --> 00:18:30.426
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:18:30.426 --> 00:18:33.710
&gt;&gt; Because that just
reinforces the accusation.

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:39.299
Well, in the virtual world,
if you have an answer to an accusation and

00:18:39.299 --> 00:18:45.932
you don't put the accusation in your
talking points, or your FAQs, or whatever,

00:18:45.932 --> 00:18:51.090
somebody searching for
that issue will never find your answer.

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:56.720
So, just using the right words so
that they are optimized for search.

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:02.531
We have to be sophisticated and know that
people are going to go to the web to find

00:19:02.531 --> 00:19:06.070
out information about
a particular issue or crisis.

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:10.770
And you gotta use the language that
they would likely use in their search.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:12.690
And so it's little things like that,

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:16.414
that I think can help us deal with
crisis management in the virtual world.

00:19:16.414 --> 00:19:21.383
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and the notion of
really thinking about wording and

00:19:21.383 --> 00:19:24.645
the exact language you use is so powerful.

00:19:24.645 --> 00:19:28.270
Most people focus on,
here's what I want to get across, but

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:32.983
if you spend just a little extra time of
thinking about, how do you use certain

00:19:32.983 --> 00:19:38.180
words to help you get that information
across, it can be incredibly powerful.

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:41.244
I joke with people,
I have a 17-year-old son,

00:19:41.244 --> 00:19:44.744
he's just gotten his driver's license,
he wants a car.

00:19:44.744 --> 00:19:46.808
You cannot, today, David, buy a used car.

00:19:46.808 --> 00:19:51.941
You can buy a certified previously owned
vehicle, but you can't buy a used car.

00:19:51.941 --> 00:19:56.672
It's the same car, but calling it
a certified previously owned makes

00:19:56.672 --> 00:20:00.857
it sound different and better and
they can charge more for it.

00:20:00.857 --> 00:20:05.022
So, this notion of choosing language
wisely to help you further your goal,

00:20:05.022 --> 00:20:08.746
I think, is really important, and
I know you and I both share that.

00:20:08.746 --> 00:20:13.701
And I hadn't thought about how
detailed you have to get in your

00:20:13.701 --> 00:20:17.333
thinking when it comes
to the virtual world.

00:20:17.333 --> 00:20:21.213
And how people actually search for
the things that you're putting out there,

00:20:21.213 --> 00:20:23.360
makes a lot of sense to me.

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:27.269
Before we end, I'd like to ask you the
same three questions I ask everyone who

00:20:27.269 --> 00:20:28.849
joins me, are you up for that?

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:34.212
&gt;&gt; I'm ready.
&gt;&gt; All right, question number one.

00:20:34.212 --> 00:20:38.574
If you were to capture the best
communication advice you ever received as

00:20:38.574 --> 00:20:42.520
a five to seven word presentation
slide title, what would it be?

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:47.440
&gt;&gt; I'm not sure I can keep it to five to
seven words, but I think I can get close.

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:48.327
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:20:48.327 --> 00:20:50.595
&gt;&gt; Worry about being right
at the end of the meeting,

00:20:50.595 --> 00:20:52.279
not at the beginning of the meeting.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:57.860
&gt;&gt; Tell me more, but I like that a lot,
I'll let you have the extra words.

00:20:57.860 --> 00:21:00.763
&gt;&gt; Well,
I think when it comes to leadership,

00:21:00.763 --> 00:21:03.675
listening is more important than talking.

00:21:03.675 --> 00:21:06.470
When you're leading a group of people, or

00:21:06.470 --> 00:21:09.888
when you're meeting with
a group of stakeholders,

00:21:09.888 --> 00:21:16.170
before you articulate your view, probably
better to listen to other people's views.

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:19.956
If you're leading a team of people and
you're in an authority role, if you lead

00:21:19.956 --> 00:21:23.757
with what you think, you can bet your
bottom dollar that people will echo that.

00:21:23.757 --> 00:21:28.026
I used to watch President Bush,
first President Bush in Cabinet meetings.

00:21:28.026 --> 00:21:31.618
He never showed his cards when he
was talking about a policy issue.

00:21:31.618 --> 00:21:36.180
He would always make the Cabinet talk
about what their views were, and

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:41.090
then he would go back to the Oval Office
and he'd make a decision.

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:44.311
If he had said, well, I don't think
this policy should be this or

00:21:44.311 --> 00:21:47.123
that, every one of those
people would be saying, Mr.

00:21:47.123 --> 00:21:50.340
President, you're so smart,
you're so brilliant.

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:53.326
That's just the nature of things, but

00:21:53.326 --> 00:21:57.230
I think the point of it is listen first,
talk next.

00:21:57.230 --> 00:22:00.990
&gt;&gt; Yes, words to live by, for sure.

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:05.500
Let me ask you question two, who is
a communicator that you admire and why?

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:12.263
&gt;&gt; I'll give you two, Gandhi and Lincoln.

00:22:12.263 --> 00:22:15.375
&gt;&gt; Okay, you can share why?

00:22:15.375 --> 00:22:20.214
&gt;&gt; Gandhi because he was able to
inspire a movement of hundreds

00:22:20.214 --> 00:22:24.589
of millions of people
against colonialism in India.

00:22:24.589 --> 00:22:28.917
And he did it in large ways
through communicating,

00:22:28.917 --> 00:22:33.247
through his book about
seeking independence, and

00:22:33.247 --> 00:22:36.277
then speaking and writing essays.

00:22:36.277 --> 00:22:41.955
And Lincoln, who, I think, had he been
able to serve out his second term,

00:22:41.955 --> 00:22:46.200
I think we would live in
a different country today.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:49.889
All you have to do is read his second
inaugural address about with malice

00:22:49.889 --> 00:22:52.020
towards none and charity for all.

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:53.327
It's a beautiful address.

00:22:53.327 --> 00:22:54.274
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:22:54.274 --> 00:22:57.342
&gt;&gt; And he did it in so few words
just like the Gettysburg Address.

00:22:57.342 --> 00:22:58.182
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:22:58.182 --> 00:23:03.296
&gt;&gt; It was so powerful,
that he was able to capture what I

00:23:03.296 --> 00:23:08.890
think was a hopeful vision of
what this country could be.

00:23:10.400 --> 00:23:16.190
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, two wonderful communicators
who did amazing things for this world.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:22.334
Last question, what are the three
ingredients, the first three ingredients,

00:23:22.334 --> 00:23:25.390
that go into a successful
communication recipe?

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:32.856
&gt;&gt; Okay, I'll be too cute by half here,
context, context, context.

00:23:32.856 --> 00:23:37.933
Knowing the contextual
environment of your circumstance,

00:23:37.933 --> 00:23:45.780
whether it's a crisis, whether it's your
reputation, whether it's your brand.

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:51.145
Having a really rigorous
understanding of the contextual

00:23:51.145 --> 00:23:56.511
environment is the foundation
of coming up with effective

00:23:56.511 --> 00:24:01.781
strategies and
achieving your organization's goals.

00:24:01.781 --> 00:24:07.518
&gt;&gt; Context is absolutely critical, as were
the topics that we discussed today, David.

00:24:07.518 --> 00:24:12.219
I can think of no one better to help us
understand how to communicate in the times

00:24:12.219 --> 00:24:16.565
we live in, than someone like yourself
who has experience in both crisis

00:24:16.565 --> 00:24:21.740
management and crisis communication,
as well as political communication.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:25.514
Thank you so much for your time, for
your insight, and for your advice.

00:24:25.514 --> 00:24:26.870
&gt;&gt; Thanks Matt.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:31.550
&gt;&gt; Thank you for listening to Think Fast,
Talk Smart, the podcast.

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:35.940
To learn more go to gsb.stanford.edu.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:40.170
Please download other episodes,
wherever you find your podcasts.

